course: english
lesson: 59
title: Olympiad: real languages and codes
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Real languages build words with rules too, and some are easier to crack than English spelling: Indonesian makes plurals by doubling (buku-buku, books), and Turkish adds an ending.
explain: Swahili changes the start of a word instead of the end: mtoto (child) becomes watoto (children), and mtu (person) becomes watu (people).
explain: When words travel into another language they are reshaped to fit its sounds: Hawaiian has no r, s or t, and its syllables end in vowels, so "Merry Christmas" became "Mele Kalikimaka"; Japanese adds vowels too, so ice cream became "aisukurīmu".
explain: Codes are puzzles with a secret rule: shift every letter one step along the alphabet (IFMMP is HELLO), or swap letters for numbers (A = 1, B = 2 and so on).
explain: Picture Ffion in Cardiff saying "cath" for cat but "fy nghath" for "my cat": Welsh changes the first sound of some words after certain little words, a change called a mutation, and checking a rule against EVERY example is how you avoid a wrong guess.
fact: In Indonesian, doubling a noun makes it plural: buku (book), buku-buku (books); rumah (house), rumah-rumah (houses).
fact: In Swahili, many words for people take m- in the singular and wa- in the plural: mtoto and watoto, mtu and watu, mpishi (cook) and wapishi (cooks).
fact: In Turkish, the plural ending is -ler when the word's last vowel is e, i, ö or ü, and -lar when it is a, ı, o or u: ev, evler (houses); kedi, kediler (cats); kitap, kitaplar (books).
fact: So Turkish gün (day) becomes günler and kız (girl) becomes kızlar; this matching of vowels is called vowel harmony.
fact: Esperanto was invented in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof; it is a real language, spoken by people around the world today.
fact: In Esperanto, mal- makes the opposite (granda big, malgranda small; varma warm, malvarma cold), and -j makes plurals, on describing words too: bela kato, belaj katoj (beautiful cats).
fact: Hawaiian has only eight consonants (there is no r, s or t, for example), and its syllables end in vowels, so borrowed words are reshaped: "Merry Christmas" became "Mele Kalikimaka".
fact: Japanese syllables almost always end in a vowel (or "n"), so borrowed words get extra vowels: "terebi" (television), "aisukurīmu" (ice cream).
fact: In Welsh, some words change their first sound after certain words; after "fy" (my), c becomes ngh, so cath (cat) becomes fy nghath (my cat).
fact: In the code where IFMMP means HELLO, every letter is shifted one step forward in the alphabet; to decode, move each letter one step back.
fact: In the code A = 1, B = 2 and so on, 3-1-20 spells CAT.
exercise: Indonesian: buku = book, buku-buku = books. If rumah = house, what does rumah-rumah mean? | Houses | A very big house | A house with a garden | Exactly two houses
exercise: Indonesian: anak = child, anak-anak = children. How is the plural made? | By saying the word twice | By adding -s to the end | By adding an- at the front | By changing its last vowel
exercise: Swahili: mtoto = child, watoto = children; mtu = person, watu = people. What is the plural of mpishi (cook)? | wapishi | mpishis | mpishi-mpishi | wampishi
exercise: Turkish: ev → evler, göz → gözler, yüz → yüzler, kitap → kitaplar, okul → okullar, balık → balıklar. What is the plural of gün (day)? | günler | günlar | güns | gün-gün
exercise: Using the same Turkish data, what is the plural of kız (girl)? | kızlar | kızler | kızs | kız-kız
exercise: In Turkish, what decides whether the plural ends in -ler or -lar? | The last vowel of the word | How long the word is | Whether it means a person | The first letter of the word
exercise: Esperanto: bona = good, malbona = bad. If varma = warm, what does malvarma mean? | Cold | Very warm | Warmer | Wet
exercise: Esperanto: granda hundo = big dog, grandaj hundoj = big dogs. If bela kato = beautiful cat, how do you say "beautiful cats"? | belaj katoj | bela katoj | belaj kato | belo katoj
exercise: Is Esperanto a real language? | Yes: invented in 1887 and spoken today | No: it only exists in science-fiction films | No: it died out completely by the year 1900 | Yes: it is the oldest language in all of Europe
exercise: Why does "Christmas" become "Kalikimaka" in Hawaiian? | Hawaiian has no r, s or t and ends syllables in vowels | Hawaiians misheard the word on purpose, as a joke | It is the name of a different festival altogether | Kalikimaka is simply the Latin word for Christmas
exercise: Code: IFMMP = HELLO. What is JTMBOE? | ISLAND | KUNCPF | HELLO | ISLE
exercise: Code: A = 1, B = 2 and so on. Decode 3-1-20. | CAT | BAT | CAR | ACT
exercise: In the same code, how do you write DOG? | 4-15-7 | 4-14-7 | 3-15-7 | 4-15-6
exercise: Welsh: cath = cat, fy nghath = my cat. Why did the c change? | Welsh changes some first sounds after "fy" | "Nghath" is a different animal from "cath" | It is a spelling mistake in the puzzle | Welsh words change spelling at random
exercise: Why does Japanese say "aisukurīmu" for ice cream? | Its syllables end in vowels, so vowels are added | Japanese speakers simply like long words | It means "very cold cream" in Japanese | It came from a misprint on an old menu
exercise: The teacher says, "Check your rule against EVERY example before writing." Why? | One example can fit a wrong rule | It is meant to waste your time | The first example is always a trick | Rules never matter in puzzles
test: Your mate says, "Swahili's like English, you just stick an -s on, innit: mtotos." Put him right. | Swahili changes the start: watoto | He is right: the plural is mtotos | It is made by doubling: mtoto-mtoto | It adds wa- in front: wamtoto
test: A cartoon says Turkish "kitap" becomes "kitapler". Fix it. | Its last vowel is a, so it takes -lar: kitaplar | Kitapler is right, as -ler goes on every word | It should simply add an -s to make kitaps | It should be doubled to make kitap-kitap
test: Esperanto: what would "malgranda hundo" mean? | Small dog | Big dog | Bad dog | Big dogs
test: Jade, Gen Z: "IFMMP is lowkey just HELLO moved one, so decode GSJFOE." ("Lowkey" means kind of.) What is it? | FRIEND | HTKGPF | FIREND | FRIENDS
test: Using the A = 1 code, what is 2-15-19-19? | BOSS | BOSE | COSS | BOTS
test: Grandad from Glasgow, dry: "Kalikimaka? Sounds like some wee eejit misheard it." What is true? | It follows Hawaiian's sound rules | He's right: it was simply misheard | It is really a Japanese word | It is really an Esperanto word
test: Indonesian: what does "anak" alone mean? | Child | Children | Many children | Childhood
test: Which of these sounds could NOT start a Hawaiian word? | s | k | m | h
test: Welsh: "fy" means my. Following the rule, if "cariad" means love, how is "my love" written? | fy nghariad | fy cariad | fy ngariad | fy hariad
test: Swahili: watu = people. What is the word for one person? | mtu | watus | wa | mtoto
test: Turkish: kedi (cat) → kediler. What is the plural of deniz (sea)? | denizler | denizlar | denizs | deniz-deniz
exam: Identify the means by which plurality of nouns is expressed in Indonesian, as exemplified by "buku-buku". | Repetition of the noun | Addition of a final -s | Alteration of the initial sound | A separate word for "many"
exam: It is given that in Swahili "mtoto" denotes "child" and "watoto" denotes "children". Determine the plural of "mpishi". | Wapishi | Mpishis | Mpishi-mpishi | Wampishi
exam: Ascertain the decoding of "JTMBOE", given that each letter of the plain text has been advanced by one place in the alphabet. | ISLAND | KUNCPF | ITLAND | ISLANE
exam: Select the response that most accurately accounts for the Hawaiian rendering "Mele Kalikimaka". | Adaptation of the phrase to Hawaiian sounds | Deliberate mistranslation for comic effect | Borrowing from the Japanese language | Translation of a different festival
